sections.s2_technical:
When you create an RDS instance you set a master username and password. That master account is administrative — it can create and drop databases, create other users, and grant any privilege. It is meant for setup and administration, not for your application's day-to-day queries.

The standard way to manage users is plain SQL on the engine. You connect once as the master to bootstrap, then create purpose-specific users with only the privileges they need.

```sql
-- Create a least-privilege user for the transfer service (PostgreSQL)
CREATE USER transfer_app WITH PASSWORD 'placeholder-will-be-rotated';
GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE ledger TO transfer_app;
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO transfer_app;
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE ON accounts, transfers TO transfer_app;
-- A read-only user for reporting — no writes at all
CREATE USER report_ro WITH PASSWORD 'placeholder-will-be-rotated';
GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE ledger TO report_ro;
GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO report_ro;
```

The transfer_app user can read and write the two tables it needs and nothing else, so even if its credentials leak, the damage is bounded. The report_ro user can only read, so a compromised reporting process can never modify money.

IAM database authentication is the token-based alternative. You create a database user and mark it to authenticate through IAM, then grant the application's IAM role the `rds-db:connect` permission for that user. At connection time the application asks IAM for a short-lived authentication token and uses it as the password.

```java
// Generate a short-lived IAM auth token instead of using a stored password
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.rds.RdsUtilities;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.rds.model.GenerateAuthenticationTokenRequest;

RdsUtilities utilities = RdsUtilities.builder().build();
String authToken = utilities.generateAuthenticationToken(
        GenerateAuthenticationTokenRequest.builder()
            .hostname("nbi-ledger.abcdef.ap-south-1.rds.amazonaws.com")
            .port(5432).username("transfer_app_iam").build());
// Use authToken as the JDBC password; it expires in ~15 minutes.
```

The big advantage is that there is no long-lived password to store or leak; the token expires quickly and access is governed by IAM, giving centralized control and a CloudTrail audit.

The reasons applications must avoid the master user are concrete. It violates least privilege, because the app gets far more power than it needs. It makes auditing meaningless, because every action looks like the same super-user and you cannot tell which service did what. It makes rotation risky, because rotating the master can break administration everywhere. And it turns one leaked credential into a total compromise, since the master can drop tables and databases. A senior nuance: I create a dedicated user per service (transfer_app, report_ro, reconciliation_app), each with the minimum grants, so permissions and blame are traceable per service, and I keep the master locked away in Secrets Manager used only for schema migrations by a controlled pipeline.

